{"path": "C:\Users\User\Desktop\agentic\3CA\Q1\report\methods.tex", "content": "\section{Methods}

\subsection{Data and metabolic feature definition}
We analyzed single-cell RNA-seq data from the Tirosh et al. melanoma study (3ca:20111), which comprises 19 samples and 4,645 cells \cite{Tirosh2016}. The raw expression matrix was provided as a transposed sparse matrix (cells $\times$ genes), with TPM values representing transcript-per-million counts. To obtain a dense feature matrix for downstream analysis, we selected 1,360 genes that met two criteria: (i) detected in at least 1\% of cells, and (ii) non-zero variance across cells. No imputation was performed; missing values were retained as zeros. The TPM values were transformed using the standard log-TPM formula:
\begin{equation}
x = \log_2(\text{TPM}/10 + 1),
\end{equation}
where $x$ is the log-transformed expression value, TPM is the raw transcript-per-million count, and the constants 10 and 1 ensure numerical stability \cite{Gavish2023}. For each gene, features were further standardized to zero mean and unit variance:
\begin{equation}
z = \frac{x - \text{mean}(x)}{\text{sd}(x)},
\end{equation}
where $z$ denotes the standardized expression, and mean and sd are the sample mean and standard deviation, respectively. The 1,360 selected genes were derived from the intersection of the study's detected genes and the KEGG scMetabolism GMT85 pathway set, which contains 85 annotated metabolic pathways \cite{Kanehisa2023}.

\subsection{PCA and clustering}
Principal component analysis (PCA) was performed on the standardized expression matrix using 50 randomized components to capture the dominant sources of variation. The first 30 principal components (PCs) were retained for downstream clustering. K-means clustering was applied to the top 30 PCs with 20 initializations (n\_init = 20) and the seed fixed at 20260912 to ensure reproducibility. Clustering was evaluated across a range of cluster numbers $k \in \{2, \dots, 10\}$ for all annotated cells, and $k \in \{2, \dots, 8\}$ for malignant cells. The silhouette coefficient was computed using a fixed subset of 2,000 cells when the total cell count exceeded 2,000; otherwise, the full cell set was used:
\begin{equation}
s = \frac{b - a}{\max(a, b)},
\end{equation}
where $a$ is the mean intra-cluster distance and $b$ is the mean inter-cluster distance \cite{Rousseeuw1987, Hubert1985}. The fitting procedure used the full dataset for all $k$ values, while the silhouette evaluation was restricted to the fixed subset to reduce computational burden.

\subsection{Matched-gene controls}
To assess the specificity of metabolic state clustering, we generated 20 random gene sets as negative controls. Each set contained 1,360 genes sampled without replacement from the full gene pool, ensuring that the total number of genes in each set matched the metabolic feature set. The random sets were drawn from the detection decile distribution without replacement, and when the number of eligible genes (detected in $\ge$1\% of cells) was insufficient, the remaining pool was used to complete the set. The PCG64 random number generator was used with a fixed seed to ensure reproducibility. For each random set, clustering was performed across the same $k$ range as for the metabolic genes, and the maximum silhouette score was recorded. No multiple testing correction was applied.

\subsection{Patient-blocked classification}
We evaluated the ability of the metabolic state model to distinguish cell types while respecting patient-level structure. A total of 4,097 annotated cells were used for classification. Cells were grouped by sample (patient) to ensure no patient contributed to both training and testing. A 5-fold cross-validation was performed using GroupKFold with 5 folds, where each fold contained cells from a single patient. Feature scaling and PCA were fitted exclusively on the training fold (PCA with 40 components). A balanced logistic regression classifier was trained with $C = 1$, max\_iter = 2,000, and balanced class weights. The balanced accuracy and macro-F1 score were computed on the held-out fold:
\begin{equation}
\text{balanced accuracy} = \frac{1}{K} \sum_{i=1}^{K} \text{recall}_i,
\end{equation}
where $K$ is the number of classes and $\text{recall}_i$ is the recall for class $i$.

\subsection{Covariate and within-patient analyses}
To test whether library complexity influenced the clustering results, we included the complexity metric (log1p-transformed) as a covariate in an ordinary least squares (OLS) regression. For the all-annotated cell analysis, complexity was included as a single covariate. For malignant cells (1,257 cells across 15 samples), we additionally included patient-level dummy variables (dropfirst) to control for sample-specific effects. The residual matrix was computed as:
\begin{equation}
R = Z - D^+ Z,
\end{equation}
where $Z$ is the standardized expression matrix, $D$ is the design matrix containing complexity and patient dummies, and $D^+$ is the Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse of $D$. The residual matrix $R$ was then used for clustering across $k \in \{2, \dots, 6\}$ for malignant cells, with non-independent validation across patients.

\subsection{Descriptive pathway summaries}
We summarized the metabolic state of each cell by computing pathway activity scores. For each cell, the expression of genes belonging to a given pathway was averaged across cells of the same cell type, and then a z-score was computed across cell types. Pathways were defined as having at least 5 genes in the KEGG scMetabolism set. Only 18 selected pathways were described in the results; these were not weighted using Xiao's method or scMetabolism scoring, nor were they derived from metabolic flux analysis. The pathway scores were computed as:
\begin{equation}
\text{pathwayscore} = \frac{\text{mean}_{\text{cell type}}(\text{mean}_{\text{gene}}(x))}{\text{sd}_{\text{cell type}}(\text{mean}_{\text{gene}}(x))},
\end{equation}
where $x$ is the log-transformed expression, and the numerator and denominator are computed across cell types.

