The reanalysis of the Tirosh melanoma single-cell RNA-seq dataset (3ca:20111) demonstrates that metabolic genes can separate cells into discrete groups, yet these groups do not correspond to established, universal malignant metabolic states. The observed separation is driven primarily by transcriptional programs that are not equivalent to metabolic fluxes. This conclusion rests on several lines of evidence and a careful appraisal of the methodological constraints inherent to the analysis.

